  "description": "item/files-versions-replace shipped, and three separate things in this run were wrong in the same way: a check that looked like proof and was not. That is the thread worth pulling, because the item itself was small and went in almost as designed.\n\nThe run opened by confirming main green after run 0014's last merge, and the confirmation was performed wrongly. main at a0cf122 reported twelve check runs where 58399dc had reported eleven, and the twelfth was pint -- from format.yml, which triggers on claude/** and ledger/** and never on main. It was there because this loop's own development branch had been reset onto main and pushed, and check runs attach to a commit rather than to a ref, so a foreign ref's workflow landed in main's check list. No wrong conclusion followed this time; both reads were genuinely green. But LOOP.md step 3 said to check that the count is complete, and a threshold like \"at least eleven\" can be satisfied entirely by another ref's checks while main's own tests run is still in flight, which is the exact error decision/0016 exists to prevent, arriving from the other direction. decision/0021 records the replacement: ask for workflow runs at the SHA, keep head_branch main and event push, require tests and ledger to conclude success. A run's conclusion already aggregates its jobs, so no count is needed at all, and a cancelled run reports cancelled instead of looking green -- which also closes the ba799a9 direction the old text described.\n\nThe item's own design was attacked before it was written, and the consultation earned its place on one point. The obvious shape for Replace was to authorise create on the directory being browsed. That is broken without any mutation: after moveFile() the selected file lives somewhere else while $this->directory is unchanged and the panel still shows it, so StoreFileVersion's (directory_id, name_key) lookup would miss and it would create a new file in the browsed directory -- the create path, reachable by an ordinary user. decision/0022 records the resolution. The directory is resolved from the file, and FilePolicy::replace() delegates to create() rather than restating files.upload plus Edit, so a future third condition on create() cannot drift away from Replace.\n\nThen the guards job rejected a mutation entry that had been written, reviewed and believed: file-policy-replace-refuses-trashed-directory STILL PASSED with its guard deleted. TrashDirectory cascades a soft delete onto every descendant file, so the cascade also trashed the file, and replace()'s trashed-FILE guard -- which runs first -- refused on its own. The directory guard was never reached by that test. This is the second run in a row where the harness rejected work already approved by review, and the shape is identical to run 0014's browser-select-directory-view: a guard that is real, a refusal that is real, and a proof that is fiction because something else refuses first. The fix isolates the directory guard by restoring the file and leaving the directory trashed, which is a reachable state rather than a contrivance, since restore(), delete() and purge() resolve the directory withTrashed() precisely so a cascade-trashed file can be restored one at a time. The trashed-file guard, which that entry had been accidentally proving all along, got an entry of its own.\n\nThe third was the container smoke, and it is decision/0023. The replace check was written with a checksum triple as its stated proof and a rendered-row count described in its own docblock as subordinate. The mutated image failed on the row count and never reached a single checksum -- and the correct build failed there too, identically, both timing out on [data-test=\"file-version-row\"].nth(1). A check that fails in both directions proves nothing whatever, and this one was four minutes from being written into the ledger as evidence. The cause was the input-draws-the-filename behaviour issue 107 already recorded: straight after an upload the main form's file input still displays the chosen name, so the page carried that text twice and getByText() did not land on the listing's link, selectFile() never fired, and there was no version list to wait for. checkTrashRemovesFileFromListingAndSearch() re-navigates before selecting for exactly this reason and has done since it was written. What run 0014 learned about a red mutation job needing its call log read, this run learned the other half of: the passing direction has to be read too, and mutation-check.php has always asserted both for guards in the suite while the smoke procedure in decision/0012 described only the failing half.\n\nThe check was then reordered so the evidence claimed to be load-bearing is the evidence that fires -- it polls the database and reports the checksum triple before touching the DOM -- and the corrected mutation names the symptom outright: \"replace did not update the document's own current version checksum\". Both runs are recorded in the docblock, correct build and mutation, because one of them alone would have been the same mistake again.\n\nTwo smaller CI findings were fixed rather than routed around, and both are the same family. A Pest expectation used toContain() on a header string, which this Pest restricts to iterables, so it raised InvalidExpectationValue and never asserted anything -- CLAUDE.md already warns about exactly this for toThrow() on an interface, and the rule generalises further than it is written. And two new phpstan findings were annotated rather than added to phpstan-baseline.neon, even though the adjacent $upload property carries the identical finding and sits in the baseline: $upload predates it, and a new baseline entry is a finding with the alarm switched off rather than one fixed.\n\nv1 is 21 of 45. Deferred deliberately and filed rather than done: issue 115, a StoreFileVersion::replace() entry point that would make the create path structurally unreachable instead of a matter of which string the caller passes -- the better design, held back because it restructures the seam every upload crosses while issue 106 is open and unexplained inside that blast radius; and issue 116, the same latent trashed-file hole in FilePolicy::update(), written down with its Livewire re-hydration premise marked explicitly unverified, because vendor/ cannot be installed here and confirming it is the first step rather than an assumption to build on. One operational limit surfaced: branch deletion is blocked from this environment -- the git proxy drops the connection on a delete refspec -- so the mutation branch could not be removed and its PR comment was corrected to say so rather than leave a false claim standing.",
